{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Ham, the father of Canaan, saw his father's nakedness.</b> He saw the humiliation that Canaan his son had done to him — that is, the castration (Sanhedrin 70a) — according to the words of some of blessed memory. And the Chaldean Berosus wrote (Genesis 6:9) that he castrated him by means of sorcery. And in his wickedness he saw and did not protest. For indeed a disgrace is called עֶרְוָה, as it says: וְעֶרְוַת מַלְכָּא לָא אֲרִיךְ לָנָא לְמֶחֱזֵי (Ezra 4:14), and likewise עֶרְוַת דָּבָר is said of a disgraceful thing (Deuteronomy 23:15)." "<b>And he told his two brothers outside.</b> For he rejoiced over his son's deed."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "Sforno cites 'the Chaldean Berosus' with the verse reference Genesis 6:9, which appears to be his source's cross-reference; rendered as given. The phrase קְצָתָם זִכְרוֹנָם לִבְרָכָה ('some of blessed memory') likely refers to certain Rabbis cited in the Talmudic passage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:29:40.110977468Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}